{
  // THE TEST FILES OF `swc-plugin-exodra`, COMPILED. The scheme is written out once in
  // `packages/core/tsconfig.tests.json`.
  //
  // ── THE ONE PACKAGE HERE THAT IS NOT TYPESCRIPT ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // `src/` is Rust; the deliverable is `swc_plugin_exodra.wasm`. `tsconfig.build.json` names this
  // package on its list of deliberate absences, and the reason it gives is exactly the hole this
  // set of files closes: "There is no TypeScript source to check; its only `.ts` file is
  // `tests/plugin.test.ts`, and tests are excluded here." There are two such files now
  // (`plugin.test.ts` and `parity.test.ts`), the sentence is still true, and the package still has
  // no tsconfig of its own — so this one extends the ROOT `tsconfig.json` and includes `tests/`
  // alone. `src/` is not in `include`, because there is nothing there a compiler could read.
  //
  // ── NO `rootDir`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // `parity.test.ts` and `plugin.test.ts` both open with
  // `import babelPlugin from '../../babel-plugin-exodra-schema/src/index'` — a RELATIVE path into
  // another package's source. That is the point of the suite: the babel plugin is the SPECIFICATION
  // and the Rust port is checked against it transform for transform, so the two must be the same
  // program rather than two builds that agree by luck. A `rootDir` would make every file it drags
  // in a TS6059, so none is set and none is inherited.
  //
  // No `paths` overrides: the Babel plugin's sources reach `@exodra/core`, `@exodra/dom` and
  // `@exodra/string` through the root config's mapping, which is why `lib` names the DOM.
  //
  // ── `files` REACHES INTO THE OTHER PACKAGE FOR ONE DECLARATION ───────────────────────────────
  //
  // `parity.test.ts` also opens `import syntaxJsx from '@babel/plugin-syntax-jsx'`, and that
  // package ships no `types` field and has no `@types/…` counterpart published, so the import
  // answers TS7016 and — the half that matters — is given an implicit `any`. The ambient
  // declaration for it lives at `packages/babel-plugin-exodra-schema/types/`, beside the four
  // suites that import the same plugin, and is named here so that ONE declaration serves both
  // programs. A second copy under this package would be two descriptions of one third-party
  // module, free to disagree.
  //
  // `files` and not `include`: this is a single known file outside the project's own directory,
  // not a pattern, and `files` says exactly that. The root config's `files: []` is inherited and
  // replaced by this line, and the two lists union — `files` names the declaration, `include`
  // still globs `tests/`. The `rootDir` note above is why a path with `..` in it is not a problem
  // here; a declaration file is exempt from the TS6059 check in any case.
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.json",
  "files": ["../babel-plugin-exodra-schema/types/babel-plugin-syntax-jsx.d.ts"],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "lib": ["ES2020", "DOM"],
    "composite": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false,
    "sourceMap": false,
    "incremental": false
  },
  "include": ["tests/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["dist", ".tsbuild", "node_modules", "target"]
}
